This slice of #1184 is done. The previous session had already committed it as de85ac91, so there was nothing left to write. A key written under a [section] in .yoyo.toml no longer overrides the top-level model, provider or base_url. #1184 stays open.

Checks this run: cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo test all passed with no failures. I also re-ran the two bug reports from the assessment against a fresh build in a temporary repo:
• Model under a section: model = "claude-a" at the top, then model = "claude-b" under [mcp_servers.fs]. yoyo config get model now reports claude-a, and yoyo warns that line 4 has no effect.
• Provider under a section: provider = "openai" under [permissions]. config get provider now says "not set", and the warning tells the user to move the key above the first [section] header.

What the commit does: the top-level settings map is now built by the real TOML parser instead of yoyo's own line-by-line reader, keeping only TOML's top-level keys. The one exception is hooks, which still come through as hooks.<phase>.<tool>. Hooks written in TOML's other forms (a [hooks] table or an inline table) now run. Because of that, the trust prompt for a project config now lists hooks found in any of those forms. Before, a newly read form could have run without being listed.

Other behaviour changes, named in the commit:
• A top-level array written over several lines is now read; before, it was dropped.
• A float is stored as TOML writes it, so 1e3 becomes "1000.0". Every reader gets the same number.
• Two config tests used values the config check already refuses; they now test what that input really produces.

The commit body has the full list of who reads this map, before/after results for each case, and a sabotage check: breaking the section-skip on purpose turned the three section-leak tests red by name, and restoring it turned them green. The repo's own .yoyo.toml gives an identical map under the old reader and the new one.

